      "spoken_text": "A hotel school organized a fermentation workshop to show students how temperature, weather, and hygiene affect a living product. The groups prepared small quantities of lactofermented vegetables with the same basic recipe, but in separately identified jars. The vegetables were weighed before salt was added. Then stuffed to stay under the brine. No bottles were considered as free tasting experiments: the main objective was to observe a controlled method and to keep a precise diary. The jars were stored in a ventilated cabinet with a temperature between 19.2 °C and 21.0 °C. On the third day, one group noticed that pieces of brine were slightly sticking out of the liquid. The jar was removed from the teaching series and the incident was discussed with the teacher, who stressed the difference between correcting a procedure and trying to save an expected result at all costs. Observation and traceability The other jars have evolved steadily. The pH. The number of people who have been exposed to the virus, measured only for demonstration purposes with a calibrated apparatus, has decreased over the days. The students compared their scores with the instrumental values without assuming that a single number could guarantee the quality of a food. They also found that the size of the pieces changed the rate at which the brine became homogeneous, even when the starting masses were close. At the end of the workshop, each group handed over a sheet with the masses, dates, observed temperatures and deviations from the protocol. The main purpose of the session was to develop a work discipline: to prepare properly. I'm going to label it. observe without unnecessary manipulation and report anomalies. The students thus approached the training as a measurable process rather than as a recipe that would succeed automatically For the next session, the teacher asked each group to prepare a blank follow-up sheet in advance This small constraint was to avoid scattered grad